cartridge "THE ENCHANTED JOURNEY/BRENT LACKIE/1985"

' THE ENCHANTED JOURNEY
' A ColecoVision game based on "The Enchanted Journey" by Brent Lackie,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette May 1985 (VIC-20 with 8K expansion, BASIC; disk
' files "journey", "y", "z"). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: someone has stolen the king's magic sword and hidden it in the
' enchanted land of dreams. Walk screen by screen through forest and castle
' (the stick, no diagonals) to find it. You have four dream chances. Before
' leaving you may take one or more magic items: Boots of Brickwalking
' (walk through brick, which crumbles behind you), Wand of Secrets (shows
' the secret doors, *), Singing Globe (a tone on each new screen, higher
' nearer the sword), Holy Cross (walk past tombstones; zombies stay still;
' the button freezes every monster, once), Wand of the Way (lights the true
' treasures and shows the true sword, stops illusions), Purple and Blue
' Keys (show the keyholes of invisible doors), Axe (chop trees), Torch
' (lights the darkness). More of them lie about the land. The sword lies
' among three deadly false swords: all four look like question marks
' unless you carry the Wand of the Way. Touch the true one to win.
'
' Kept from the listing (orig/z.bas): the 40-screen map (which walls,
' doors, secret doors, keyholes and inside walls each screen has, the
' forest and graveyard screens, the dark and the shaking screens, the
' exits: right +1, left -1, down -8, up +8, and the screens 33, 34 and 35
' at the map's ends; arriving in a wall throws you to the forest, screen
' 33), trees that grow anew on every visit, 15-29 tombstones and 13
' zombies in graveyards, the four fluffers (in three wandering screens,
' one of them steps each turn), the brick monster (leaves brick behind,
' stopped by trees and tombstones), the creeping eyes (stopped by brick,
' eat what they cross), the bobcat (two cells wide, always moves
' diagonally, passes everything), the blue thief (steals one item, then is
' gone), the yellow disease (reverses the stick; dying cures it), the
' globe of lies (sings backwards; the true globe overrides it), the
' illusions (a false globe, axe and boots), the transporter, the strange
' door of screen 16 (gives a random treasure once), wandering monsters
' that drift towards you between screens, falling bricks in the shaking
' rooms, the cross that looks like a tombstone, the start menu (forest,
' random room, choose treasures with 0 = random) and four lives.
'
' Liberties: the hero glides a cell in 8 frames and the monsters step
' every 18 frames and wait 1 second after you arrive on a screen (in the BASIC they moved as often as you did); a falling
' brick shows a crack for 20 frames before it lands and never lands on a
' door; forests grow 30-59 trees (the listing 50-99: without monsters
' eating them, so many trees could wall you in); treasures are visible without the torch (the listing drew them
' only when the torch was carried) except in dark screens, where the
' whole screen is black but the hero; the sword, the treasures, a respawn
' and every teleport go to screens that can be reached from the forest
' without treasures (16 of the 40; the listing allowed sealed screens);
' the frozen monsters stay frozen (the listing let them drift back); the
' bobcat drifts towards you like the others (the listing always lowered
' its screen); the singing globe sings highest on the sword's own screen;
' holding the button 1 second without the cross ends the quest (one
' press did); the hero stays visible in the dark; the item menu is driven
' by the stick. Monsters no longer erase the scenery they cross (the
' listing poked a blank behind them): an underlay of the room's background
' in spare VRAM puts back trees, tombstones, doors, cracks and treasures
' behind every actor; only the brick monster still leaves brick, and the
' hero only clears what he uses up (a chopped tree, crossed brick, a
' passed tombstone, a treasure taken).
'
' Controls: the stick moves (diagonals do nothing, as in the original);
' either button: freeze the monsters with the cross, or hold 1 second to
' give up. On the menus: up/down choose, a button selects.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22 (the play grid is columns
' 3-24, rows 1-22, the panel 26-29); tiles from code 128; screens drawn
' with the NMI off after a wait, display off; music first in the sound
' table; the title tune played once; nothing flashes.

